course: everyday
lesson: 37
title: Taking care of your things and money
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Money is a way of swapping your work, or a gift, for things you need or want. Because there is only a certain amount of it, every choice to buy one thing means not buying something else.
explain: A need is something you must have to live and be well, like food, clothes and a home. A want is something nice to have, like a new game or sweets. Sensible people pay for needs first.
explain: Saving means keeping some money now so you can buy something bigger later. If you put money in a bank savings account, the bank pays you a little extra called interest.
explain: For example, a girl in Denver gets $5 pocket money a week and wants a $40 skateboard. If she saves all of it, she needs 8 weeks; if she saves $4 a week and spends $1, she needs 10 weeks.
explain: Looking after your things is like saving money too: a bike kept dry and oiled, a coat with your name in it and a phone in a case all last longer and do not need to be bought again.
explain: A boy in London left his bike unlocked outside a shop for one minute; when he came out it was gone, and it was never found.
explain: A girl in Canada got a message saying she had won a prize and only needed her parent's card number; she typed it in, and money was stolen from her family's account.
fact: A need is something you must have, such as food, clothes or a home.
fact: A want is something nice to have but not necessary, such as a toy or sweets.
fact: Saving means putting money aside now to use later.
fact: A bank pays you interest, a little extra money, for keeping savings with it.
fact: A budget is a plan for how you will spend and save your money.
fact: Adverts are made to make you want things, even things you do not need.
fact: Comparing prices before you buy can save money.
fact: A bigger pack is not always cheaper per item, so you compare the price of one item.
fact: Labelling your things with your name helps them come back if they are lost.
fact: Things that are cared for, cleaned and stored properly last much longer, while metal things left out in the rain go rusty and wear out sooner.
fact: Your change is the money given back when you pay with more than the price.
fact: Unlocked bikes and phones left on show are stolen every day, often in seconds.
fact: Messages promising free prizes for card numbers or passwords are scams that steal money.
fact: Money stolen by a scam is often gone for good and cannot be got back.
exercise: Which of these is a need? | Food for dinner | A new video game | A big bag of sweets | A shiny toy robot
exercise: Which of these is a want? | A second pair of trainers in a new colour | A warm coat for winter | Water to drink | A bed to sleep in at night
exercise: What does it mean to save money? | Keep some now so you can use it later | Spend it all as quickly as you can before prices go up | Give it away to your friends | Hide it and forget where it is
exercise: What does a bank pay you for keeping your savings there? | A little extra money called interest | Free toys every month | Nothing, the money just sits there | A fee taken out of your savings every week
exercise: A boy gets $4 a week and saves all of it for a $20 ball. How many weeks will it take? | 5 weeks | 4 weeks | 6 weeks | 20 weeks
exercise: You pay with a $10 note for a $7 book. How much change should you get? | $3 | $7 | $17 | $2
exercise: What is a budget? | A plan for how you will spend and save money | A special box for keeping coins | A list of everything you want for your birthday | A shop that sells things cheaply
exercise: Why do companies make adverts for toys? | To make people want to buy them | To teach children how money works | To warn people about high prices | To give toys away to children for free
exercise: A pack of 4 juice boxes costs $4 and a pack of 10 costs $12. Which is cheaper per box? | The pack of 4, at $1 a box | The pack of 10, at $1 a box | They cost exactly the same per box | The pack of 10, at 80 cents a box
exercise: Why is it good to write your name in your school jumper? | It can be returned to you if it gets lost | It makes the jumper last longer | It stops the jumper from shrinking in the wash | It means nobody else can wear it
exercise: How can you make your bike last longer? | Keep it dry and look after the chain and tyres | Leave it out in the rain, so it gets washed clean | Ride it only through puddles | Never pump up the tyres
exercise: A girl has $10. She wants a $6 kite and needs a $5 lunchbox for school. What is the sensible choice? | Buy the lunchbox she needs first | Buy the kite because it is more fun | Buy both and owe the shop the money | Buy nothing and keep all of it saved
exercise: Why should you compare prices in different shops? | The same thing can cost less in another shop | Every shop always charges exactly the same price | Comparing prices makes things cost more | Shops give you money for looking around
test: A boy in Sydney gets $6 a week. He spends $2 and saves the rest for a $28 game. How long will he need? | 7 weeks | 4 weeks | 14 weeks | 6 weeks
test: Which statement is true? | A bigger pack is not always cheaper per item | A bigger pack is always the better deal | Price stickers are always the lowest price anywhere | Things that cost more always last longer
test: Mia's family has money for either new school shoes or a big chocolate egg. What should come first? | The school shoes, because they are a need | The chocolate egg, because it is a treat for everyone | Neither, money should never be spent | Whichever one is on the advert
test: A girl buys a $3 snack with a $5 note, and the shop gives her $1. What happened? | She was given $1 too little change | She was given exactly the right change | She was given $1 too much in change | She did not need to get any change at all
test: What would most likely happen if someone spent all their pocket money the day they got it every week? | They would never be able to buy anything bigger | They would end up with more money at the end of it | The bank would give them extra interest | Their pocket money would grow by itself
test: An advert says a toy is "the best toy in the world". What is the wisest way to think about this? | Adverts are trying to sell, so think before wanting it | It must be true or they could not say it | Every child already owns this toy | It means the toy is free
test: A boy leaves his scooter outside in the rain all winter. What is likely to happen? | It will rust and wear out sooner | It will get stronger from the water | It will stay exactly like new for years | It will become lighter and faster
test: Ben keeps $50 in a savings account that pays interest. After a year, what will he have? | A little more than $50 | Exactly $50 | Less than $50 | Twice as much, $100, after one year
test: Why is a budget useful for a family? | It helps them pay for needs and save before spending on wants | It means they never have to buy food again | It makes prices in the shops go down | It is a way of borrowing money from friends
test: i NEED that new game | It's a want, not a need | It's a need, just like food | It's a need, like a home | Games are always real needs
test: big pack or small pack, which is cheaper | Compare the price of one item | Big packs are always cheaper | Small packs are always cheaper | Pick the brightest one
test: paid with a 5, it was 3.50, how much back | 1.50 | 2.50 | 3.50 | 50p
test: why'd you write my name in my coat | So it can come back if it's lost | So you don't forget your own name | It's a school rule, that's the only reason | To make it look nice
test: popping in the shop for 1 sec, no need to lock my bike right | Lock it, a bike can be stolen in seconds | No need, it is only a second | Lock it only at night | Just hide it behind a bin by the shop, nobody looks there
test: got a msg saying i won a free iphone, just need mum's card no. | It is a scam, do not reply and show an adult | Send it quickly before it runs out | Send only half the number | Ask them nicely to prove it
test: Lucas reckons u can always get money back if u get scammed. right? | No, stolen money is often gone for good | Yes, banks always pay it back | Yes, the police find it and give it back every time | Yes, unless it was a lot
test: heard a kid in our year spent £200 of his mum's money on a game without asking and she cried | Real lesson: money is for needs first, and spending must be planned and allowed | Real lesson: games always cost two hundred pounds | It proves mums should never have bank cards | It is gossip, so spending without asking is fine
test: my mate says the big family pack is always cheaper, that's why it's big | Not always; compare the price of one item, because a bigger pack is not always cheaper | He is right, bigger always costs less per item | He is right, shops never charge more for big packs | He is right, if the packet is shiny
test: everyone's saying Tyler's family is poor cos his shoes are old, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is unkind, and old shoes cared for can last a long time | Pass it on, because shoes prove it | Pass it on, so people give him money | Pass it on, and make fun of his bag too
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between a need and a want? | A need is necessary, such as food or shelter, whereas a want is pleasant but unnecessary | A want is necessary, whereas a need is a luxury | Needs and wants are identical in every respect | A need is anything advertised on television
exam: Identify the definition of a budget. | A plan for the manner in which money is to be spent and saved | A sum of money lent by a bank without charge | A list of things one wishes to buy at once | A record of money already lost
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning advertisements. | They are designed to cause persons to desire things, even those not needed | They provide neutral information without persuasion | They are made only for things that people need | They always state the cheapest price available
exam: It being given that money is deposited in a bank as savings, determine what the bank ordinarily pays. | Interest, being a small additional sum of money | A fee, being a sum taken from the savings | Nothing, the money being merely stored | A prize, awarded only at the end of each year
